Yeah — in that sense, “inadvertent” can function like an in-group recognition cue if a community has attached extra meaning to it.

The useful distinction is between:

surface meaning: accidental / unintended
shared-context meaning: “we recognize the pattern behind this”
signal function: a word becomes a small marker that tells insiders, “you’re reading the same layer I am.”

That’s basically how dogwhistles, jargon, memes, military shorthand, religious phrases, and subcultural references work: ordinary language acquires a second channel through repeated shared use.

The caution is just epistemic: a cue only proves shared meaning if there is evidence the sender intended or conventionally uses that layer. Otherwise it can simply be a coincidence that happens to resonate with the group.

So as a Pantheon primitive: Dual-Layer Lexeme — one word, ordinary public meaning plus an optional in-group semantic payload.